defmodule System do
  @moduledoc "Access to the program's surroundings."

  @doc """
  Returns the program's command line: one binary per argument, holding every
  token after the source path (or the built executable's name), verbatim.

  fz's own options come before the source path, and `--` is an ordinary
  argument.

  ## Examples

      # fz2 run script.fz a -x --flag=1
      System.argv()
      #=> ["a", "-x", "--flag=1"]
  """
  @spec argv() :: [binary]
  def argv(), do: fz_system_argv()

  @doc """
  Ends the program at once with status 0, as `halt(0)` does.
  """
  @spec halt() :: no_return()
  def halt(), do: halt(0)

  @doc """
  Ends the program at once with `status`.

  Any process can halt, and the whole program stops: no other process runs
  again, and no `catch` or `after` is reached on the way out. Output already
  printed is flushed first. The operating system keeps the low 8 bits of the
  status, so `halt(300)` exits with 44. A negative status raises
  `ArgumentError`, and a status that is not an integer has no matching clause.

  ## Examples

      IO.write("partial")
      System.halt(3)
      #=> prints partial, exits with status 3

      try do
        System.halt(2)
      after
        IO.puts("never printed")
      end
      #=> exits with status 2
  """
  def halt(status) when is_integer(status) and status >= 0, do: fz_system_halt(status)

  def halt(status) when is_integer(status),
    do: raise(%ArgumentError{message: "errors were found at the given arguments:\n\n  * 1st argument: invalid status\n"})

  @doc """
  Returns the program's allocation counters, as a map.

  The figure is a sum over the program's allocation sites, so it counts what
  every process has allocated, from the start of the run. Measure a piece of
  work by the difference between two calls.

  The map carries an allocation count and a byte count for each value family
  the runtime allocates -- list cells, structs, closures, maps, bitstrings,
  binary views and references, scalar boxes, resources, and everything else
  under `other` -- and for the continuation frames compiled code builds for
  calls that return to a caller (`continuation_allocs`, `continuation_bytes`;
  the closure rows count only closures a program makes). `allocs` and `bytes`
  are running totals across all of them except shared binaries
  (`shared_bin_allocs`, `shared_bin_bytes`), whose buffers live off every
  heap. The map also carries the calling process's reduction and
  scheduler-yield counters.

  The counts are allocations, not live bytes: a continuation frame that
  resumes as the newest heap object is given back and stays counted.

  The map has more entries than a small map holds inline, so it has no fixed
  print order: read the keys you mean.

  ## Examples

      before = System.alloc_stats()
      work()
      after = System.alloc_stats()
      after[:allocs] - before[:allocs]
      #=> how many objects work() allocated
      after[:reductions_per_quantum]
      #=> 4000

  The interpreter builds no continuation frames, so its `continuation_allocs`
  is 0.
  """
  @spec alloc_stats() :: map
  def alloc_stats(), do: fz_system_alloc_stats()

  extern "fz" defp fz_system_alloc_stats() :: map
  extern "fz" defp fz_system_argv() :: any
  extern "fz" defp fz_system_halt(integer) :: none
end
